				            </tr>

				            <tr>
				                <td>Suitability</td>
				                <td>Typically higher-investment-capacity investors</td>
				                <td>Wide range of investors</td>
				            </tr>
				        </tbody>
				    </table>
				</div>

				<p>
				    Neither product should automatically be considered superior. The right choice depends on
				    the investor's goals, risk profile, investment amount, time horizon and preferences.
				</p>


				<h2>Why Do Investors Consider PMS?</h2>

				<p>There are several reasons an investor may consider PMS.</p>


				<h3>1. Personalized Portfolio Management</h3>

				<p>
				    One of the biggest attractions of PMS is the possibility of having a portfolio managed
				    according to a defined strategy for the individual client.
				</p>


				<h3>2. Direct Ownership of Securities</h3>

				<p>
				    PMS portfolios can involve direct ownership of securities in the client's account,
				    depending on the strategy and structure.
				</p>

				<p>
				    This can provide greater visibility into the individual holdings.
				</p>


				<h3>3. Professional Management</h3>

				<p>
				    Investors who don't have the time or expertise to continuously monitor markets may prefer
				    professional portfolio management.
				</p>


				<h3>4. Focused Investment Strategies</h3>

				<p>
				    Some PMS strategies may focus on particular approaches such as:
				</p>

				<ul>
				    <li>Quality investing</li>
				    <li>Value investing</li>
				    <li>Growth investing</li>
				    <li>Special situations</li>
				    <li>Small and mid-cap opportunities</li>
				    <li>Multicap strategies</li>
				    <li>Concentrated portfolios</li>
				</ul>

				<p>
				    The exact strategy varies from one Portfolio Manager to another.
				</p>


				<h2>Is PMS Suitable for Every Investor?</h2>

				<p>
				    <strong>No.</strong> This is one of the most important points to understand.
				</p>

				<p>
				    PMS is not simply a product for someone who wants "higher returns." Equity-oriented PMS
				    strategies can involve significant market risk. A concentrated portfolio can also experience
				    larger movements than a diversified portfolio.
				</p>

				<p>An investor considering PMS should think about:</p>

				<ul>
				    <li>How much money can I invest?</li>
				    <li>Can I tolerate substantial temporary losses?</li>
				    <li>What is my investment horizon?</li>
				    <li>Do I understand the strategy?</li>
				    <li>Do I need the money in the near future?</li>
				    <li>Am I comfortable with portfolio volatility?</li>
				    <li>Have I reviewed the fees and other charges?</li>
				    <li>Am I choosing the manager based on process rather than only historical returns?</li>
				</ul>

				<p>
				    If the answer to these questions is unclear, the investor should spend more time
				    understanding the product before investing.
				</p>


				<h2>PMS Does Not Guarantee Returns</h2>

				<p>
				    This point cannot be emphasized enough. PMS is an investment management service,
				    not a guaranteed-return product.
				</p>

				<p>
				    Market conditions can change quickly. Even an experienced Portfolio Manager can make
				    incorrect investment decisions or face periods of underperformance.
				</p>

				<p>
				    SEBI's PMS framework requires fees to be agreed with clients and does not permit Portfolio
				    Managers to guarantee or assure returns.
				</p>

				<p>Therefore, investors should be cautious of statements such as:</p>

				<ul>
				    <li>"Guaranteed monthly returns"</li>
				    <li>"Fixed 20% return"</li>
				    <li>"No-loss strategy"</li>
				    <li>"100% accurate market calls"</li>
				    <li>"Guaranteed double money"</li>
				</ul>

				<p>
				    Promises like these should immediately raise questions.
				</p>


				<h2>How Are PMS Fees Charged?</h2>

				<p>
				    PMS fees vary depending on the Portfolio Manager and the selected strategy.
				</p>

				<p>
				    Depending on the arrangement, fees can include different structures, such as:
				</p>

				<ul>
				    <li>Fixed management fees</li>
				    <li>Performance-linked fees</li>
				    <li>A combination of fee structures</li>
				</ul>

				<p>
				    The exact fee structure should be clearly understood before investing.
				</p>

				<p>
				    Don't look at the management fee alone. An investor should understand the complete cost
				    structure, including applicable expenses and charges associated with portfolio management
				    and transactions.
				</p>

				<p>
				    SEBI's regulations require Portfolio Managers to disclose the range of fees charged under
				    various heads in the disclosure document.
				</p>


				<h2>What Risks Should PMS Investors Understand?</h2>

				<p>
				    PMS can offer a personalized investment approach, but personalization does not remove
				    investment risk.
				</p>


				<h3>Market Risk</h3>

				<p>
				    If the market falls, the value of the portfolio can decline.
				</p>


				<h3>Concentration Risk</h3>

				<p>
				    Some PMS strategies may hold a relatively concentrated set of securities. If a major
				    holding performs poorly, it can have a significant impact on the portfolio.
				</p>


				<h3>Liquidity Risk</h3>

				<p>
				    Certain securities may not be easy to buy or sell at the desired price.
				</p>


				<h3>Strategy Risk</h3>

				<p>
				    A particular investment philosophy may perform well during one market environment and
				    struggle during another.
				</p>


				<h3>Manager Risk</h3>

				<p>
				    The Portfolio Manager's investment process, research quality and decision-making can
				    materially affect results.
				</p>


				<h3>Volatility Risk</h3>

				<p>
				    Equity-focused portfolios can experience significant short-term fluctuations.
				</p>

				<p>
				    This is why investors should evaluate PMS based on risk-adjusted performance, consistency,
				    process and suitability, rather than looking only at the highest return number.
				</p>


				<h2>How Should You Evaluate a PMS?</h2>

				<p>
				    Choosing a PMS should involve more than asking:
				    <strong>"How much return did you make last year?"</strong>
				</p>


				<h3>1. Check SEBI Registration</h3>

				<p>
				    Verify that the entity is a registered Portfolio Manager. SEBI provides a current
				    registered Portfolio Manager database for investors to check.
				</p>


				<h3>2. Understand the Investment Philosophy</h3>

				<p>
				    Ask: <strong>"How do you select stocks?"</strong>
				</p>

				<p>
				    A good explanation should be understandable and consistent with the documented strategy.
				</p>


				<h3>3. Study Performance Over Different Periods</h3>

				<p>
				    Don't focus exclusively on one exceptional year. Look at performance across different
				    market environments and understand how the strategy behaved during market corrections.
				</p>


				<h3>4. Understand Downside Risk</h3>

				<p>
				    Ask how the strategy performed during difficult markets. A portfolio's ability to manage
				    downside risk can be just as important as its ability to generate returns.
				</p>


				<h3>5. Understand the Fees</h3>

				<p>
				    Know exactly what you are paying and how performance-linked fees, if applicable,
				    are calculated.
				</p>


				<h3>6. Understand the Portfolio</h3>

				<p>Ask:</p>

				<ul>
				    <li>How many stocks are normally held?</li>
				    <li>What is the typical allocation?</li>
				    <li>How much concentration is allowed?</li>
				    <li>What is the investment horizon?</li>
				    <li>How frequently is the portfolio reviewed?</li>
				</ul>


				<h3>7. Read the Documentation</h3>

				<p>
				    Don't invest based solely on a presentation, phone call or WhatsApp message.
				    Review the relevant documents and disclosures before making a decision.
				</p>


				<h2>PMS vs Direct Stock Investing</h2>

				<p>
				    Some investors wonder:
				    <strong>"If PMS invests in stocks, why don't I just buy the stocks myself?"</strong>
				</p>

				<p>
				    That is a fair question. The difference is often the process and professional management.
				</p>

				<p>
				    An individual investor has to conduct research, track company developments, monitor
				    valuations, manage portfolio allocation and decide when to exit.
				</p>

				<p>
				    A professional PMS team may have dedicated research resources and a defined investment process.
				    However, professional management does not guarantee better performance.
				</p>

				<p>
				    If you have the knowledge, time and discipline to manage your own portfolio, direct investing
				    may be suitable for you.
				</p>

				<p>
				    If you prefer professional management and have the required investment capacity,
				    PMS may be worth evaluating.
				</p>


				<h2>PMS Performance: What Should Investors Look At?</h2>

				<p>
				    Performance should always be viewed in context.
				</p>

				<p>
				    Suppose one PMS generated 30% in one year while another generated 18%. It would be tempting
				    to immediately choose the 30% strategy. But that doesn't tell the complete story.
				</p>

				<p>You should also consider:</p>

				<ul>
				    <li>Benchmark performance</li>
				    <li>Volatility</li>
				    <li>Drawdowns</li>
				    <li>Consistency</li>
				    <li>Investment horizon</li>
				    <li>Portfolio concentration</li>
				    <li>Risk-adjusted performance</li>
				    <li>Investment strategy</li>
				</ul>

				<p>
				    A strategy generating high returns with significantly higher risk may not necessarily be
				    the better choice for every investor.
				</p>

				<p>
				    <strong>Returns tell you what happened. Risk and process help explain how it happened.</strong>
				</p>


				<h2>How Much Should You Allocate to PMS?</h2>

				<p>
				    There is no universal percentage that every investor should allocate to PMS.
				    It depends on factors such as:
				</p>

				<ul>
				    <li>Total net worth</li>
				    <li>Income stability</li>
				    <li>Existing investments</li>
				    <li>Financial goals</li>
				    <li>Emergency fund</li>
				    <li>Risk tolerance</li>
				    <li>Investment horizon</li>
				    <li>Liquidity requirements</li>
				</ul>

				<p>
				    An investor should not put money required for near-term expenses into a high-risk
				    investment strategy simply because the historical returns look attractive.
				</p>

				<p>
				    Before considering PMS, it is sensible to have adequate liquidity for emergencies
				    and important financial commitments.
				</p>


				<h2>Common Mistakes Investors Make With PMS</h2>


				<h3>Mistake 1: Choosing Based Only on Past Returns</h3>

				<p>
				    Past performance does not guarantee future results.
				</p>


				<h3>Mistake 2: Ignoring Risk</h3>

				<p>
				    A high-return strategy may also involve high volatility.
				</p>


				<h3>Mistake 3: Not Understanding the Investment Philosophy</h3>

				<p>
				    If you cannot explain how the PMS generates returns, you may not fully understand
				    what you are investing in.
				</p>


				<h3>Mistake 4: Ignoring Fees</h3>

				<p>
				    Fees can affect your overall investment outcome.
				</p>


				<h3>Mistake 5: Expecting Monthly Returns</h3>

				<p>
				    Equity markets do not move in a predictable monthly pattern.
				</p>


				<h3>Mistake 6: Investing Because Someone Recommended It</h3>

				<p>
				    A recommendation from a friend, colleague or salesperson should not replace your own
				    due diligence.
				</p>


				<h3>Mistake 7: Panicking During Corrections</h3>

				<p>
				    Equity strategies can experience periods of underperformance. Investors should understand
				    the expected risk before investing.
				</p>


				<h2>Questions to Ask Before Investing in PMS</h2>

				<p>
				    Before signing up, consider asking the Portfolio Manager:
				</p>

				<ol>
				    <li>What is the investment philosophy?</li>
				    <li>What type of investor is this strategy designed for?</li>
				    <li>What is the expected investment horizon?</li>
				    <li>What is the typical portfolio size?</li>
				    <li>How concentrated can the portfolio become?</li>
				    <li>What are the major risks?</li>
				    <li>What are the complete fees and charges?</li>
				    <li>How is performance reported?</li>
				    <li>What benchmark is used?</li>
				    <li>How did the strategy perform during major market corrections?</li>
				    <li>How often is the portfolio reviewed?</li>
				    <li>What is the process for buying and selling securities?</li>
				    <li>What disclosures will I receive?</li>
				    <li>What happens if I want to withdraw money?</li>
				    <li>Is the Portfolio Manager currently registered with SEBI?</li>
				</ol>

				<p>
				    These questions can help you make a more informed decision.
				</p>


				<h2>PMS in India: A Growing Investment Segment</h2>

				<p>
				    PMS has become an important part of India's professional investment-management ecosystem.
				    SEBI's latest available PMS statistics show that hundreds of Portfolio Managers operate
				    in the Indian market, with a large number of clients and substantial assets managed across
				    discretionary, non-discretionary, co-investment and advisory categories.
				</p>

				<p>
				    As of <strong>July 31, 2026</strong>, SEBI reported 514 PMS submissions and total reported
				    assets of about <strong>₹44.11 lakh crore</strong> across the categories covered in its
				    statistics, including significant institutional assets.
				</p>

				<p>
				    These figures show that PMS is not a niche concept anymore. However, the size of the
				    industry should not be interpreted as evidence that PMS is suitable for every investor.
				    Suitability still comes first.
				</p>


				<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

				<p>
				    PMS can be an interesting option for investors who have a higher investment capacity,
				    understand market risks and want professional portfolio management with a more personalized
				    approach.
				</p>

				<p>
				    But PMS should not be viewed as a shortcut to extraordinary returns.
				</p>

				<p>The right way to approach PMS is to ask:</p>

				<ul>
				    <li>Who is managing my money?</li>
				    <li>What is their investment process?</li>
				    <li>What risks am I taking?</li>
				    <li>What am I paying?</li>
				    <li>Does this strategy actually fit my financial goals?</li>
				</ul>

				<p>
				    If you can answer these questions clearly, you are in a much better position to evaluate
				    whether PMS belongs in your investment journey.
				</p>

				<p>
				    Ultimately, successful investing is not about finding the product with the highest
				    advertised return. It is about choosing an investment approach that you understand,
				    can afford to hold through different market conditions, and that fits your long-term
				    financial objectives.
				</p>


